title Forty names the tree no longer shares
prompt Keaton -- migrate a first batch to pens and lower the ceiling
think Each run in a Rishi witness is its own shell, so a pen made in one does not survive to the next. That ruled out the obvious transform and sent me looking for a smaller one.
think Found it by probing rather than assuming: a captured .out keeps its trailing newline and breaks the path, and printf %s around the capture trims it. That turned a per-file rewrite into a three-line mechanical edit good for 1,085 of the 1,224 files.
obs BASELINED BEFORE MIGRATING. All forty lattice witnesses green first, so any red afterward would be mine rather than pre-existing. Forty green after, so no regression.
obs THE CONCURRENCY EVIDENCE IS HONEST RATHER THAN TIDY. On this fast three-second witness I could not reliably reproduce the fault: the elder shape answered 16 of 16 RED on one trial and then 0 red across 112 later runs, and the migrated shape carried 1 red in 112. One wipeout is consistent with a shared name -- all sixteen failed together rather than one -- and one trial is not a proof, so I am not claiming this batch proved the mechanism.
obs WHAT IS PROVEN sits on %700's own witness earlier today, whose build takes sixteen seconds and therefore holds the window open: elder 3 green and 5 red at eight concurrent, repaired 8 green and 0 red. The lattice batch rests on that plus the structural removal of forty shared names.
obs THE METER WENT BLIND TO ITS OWN REPAIR and I caught it by reading the count rather than trusting the drop. A migrated site names its pen through a SECOND binding, so sites_penned held at 6 while the fixed count fell by forty -- the forty had become unresolved. Teaching the scan one more level took penned to 46, and three control legs now hold that behavior.
obs Had I not looked, every future migration would have moved sites into unresolved and read as work that merely made the tree harder to measure.
loom batch files=40 green_before=40 green_after=40 sites_removed=40
